FamilyTree.person({"id":"cornelia_verkouille","name":"Cornelia Coleta Verkouille","dates":"11 Oct 1836 Oostende – 6 Apr 1910 Oostende","confidence":"sup","surname":"Verkouille","family":"verkouille","born":"11 Oct 1836 Oostende","order":"1836-10-11","died":"6 Apr 1910 Oostende","occupation":"werkster (charwoman)","branch":"Devriendt","line":"devriendt","father":"livinus_verkouille","mother":"maria_malfait","spouses":[{"id":"carolus_ramon","name":"Carolus Ludovicus Ramon","detail":"m. Oostende, 14 Sep 1859"}],"source":"Geneanet","note":"Daughter of Livinus Verkouille & Maria Malfait; widowed in 1883 when her fisherman husband was lost at sea, she outlived him by 27 years."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"cornelius_bossin","name":"Cornelius Bossin","dates":"b. Sint-Stevens-Woluwe","confidence":"sup","surname":"Bossin","family":"bossin","born":"Sint-Stevens-Woluwe","branch":"Thumas","line":"thumas","father":"guilielmus_bossin","mother":"peremans","source":"Birth declarations at Sint-Stevens-Woluwe for three children of Guilielmus Bossin x Peremans, 1847-1853","note":"Eldest known child of [[guilielmus_bossin]] and [[peremans]], and an elder brother of\n[[bossin]]. His birth was declared at Sint-Stevens-Woluwe on **13 September 1847**, eighteen\nmonths after his parents married at Zaventem.\n\nThe birth entry is a `raw` note rather than a date because the index gives the **declaration**,\nnot the birth. They are usually a day or two apart, and this project does not turn one into\nthe other.\n\n`sup`: an index row, no register image read. What makes it safe rather than a guess is the\nsibling in the middle of the same series — his sister's declaration of 11 January 1849 sits\nagainst a birth of 10 January 1849 already read from her marriage act, with the same two\nparents and the same commune (S24).\n\nFRONTIER: his own birth act, for the exact date; and any marriage or death — nothing is known\nof him after 1847."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"cornelius_peremans","name":"Cornelius Peremans","dates":"","confidence":"sup","surname":"Peremans","family":"peremans","sex":"m","branch":"Thumas","line":"thumas","father":"egidius_peremans","mother":"joanna_verelst","source":"Marriage banns of Cornelius Peremans, Zaventem, 21 April 1844","note":"A second sibling for [[peremans]]. His marriage banns were published at **Zaventem on 21 April\n1844** (S30), naming his parents as [[egidius_peremans]] and *Theresia Verelst* — the short\nform of [[joanna_verelst]]'s name, which is how half this family's acts write her.\n\nThe timing is worth noticing. His father had been dead seven years and his mother **four\nmonths** — she died on 19 December 1843. He married in the spring after burying her, and his\nsister [[peremans]] married in the February after that, at twenty-one and orphaned on both\nsides.\n\nAnchored the same way as [[mjosephina_peremans]]: the record itself gives no place, and the\ncommune comes from the title of the volume the page sits in. Zaventem is where both his parents\ndied and where two of his sisters married.\n\n**His wife is not recorded, deliberately.** The index lists three more people in the same\ndocument — *Lambertus van der Pelen*, *Maria Catharina van der Vaeren* and *Maria Anna van der\nVaeren* — and states the role of none of them. Maria Anna is the likeliest bride and the other\ntwo the likeliest parents, but the surnames do not line up in the obvious way, and guessing\nwhich of three unlabelled people someone married is exactly the kind of invention this project\nforbids.\n\nFRONTIER: the marriage act itself, which would name the bride and both sets of parents; and his\nown birth act at Zaventem, probably in the 1810s or 1820s."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"cornelius_vandewalle","name":"Cornelius Vandewalle","dates":"","confidence":"sup","surname":"Vandewalle","family":"vandewalle","sex":"m","branch":"Bundervoet","line":"bostyn-cappaert","spouses":[{"id":"maria_vanrenterghem","name":"Maria Van Renterghem"}],"source":"isavdw — Rijksarchief-sourced, with scan links","note":"Earlier husband of [[maria_vanrenterghem]], who afterwards married [[michael_rups]]\n— our line runs through that second marriage, via their daughter [[maria_rups]].\n\nA Vandewalle marrying into this family is not a coincidence worth ignoring: the\nVandewalles appear again a generation on, when Maria Rups married\n[[carolus_vandewalle]] at Oostkamp in 1662. FRONTIER — whether Cornelius and\nCarolus are of the same family. Nothing here establishes it, and the surname alone\nis not evidence."});
