FamilyTree.person({"id":"egidius_peremans","name":"Egidius Peremans","dates":"d. 6 Mar 1837 Zaventem","confidence":"doc","surname":"Peremans","family":"peremans","died":"6 Mar 1837 Zaventem","branch":"Thumas","line":"thumas","spouses":[{"id":"joanna_verelst","name":"Joanna Theresia Ver Elst"}],"source":"Zaventem marriage act nr. 1, 24 February 1846 — Bossin × Peremans","note":"Father of the bride in his daughter [[peremans]]'s marriage act — Zaventem, act nr. 1,\n24 February 1846 (S19), read as an image. He was dead by then, and the act gives the date\nand place exactly: Zaventem, 6 March 1837. His wife [[joanna_verelst]] outlived him by six\nyears.\n\nThe act gives him no age and no occupation. His daughter was 21 in February 1846, which\nplaces his own birth loosely in the 1780s or 1790s; nothing narrower is recorded.\n\nFRONTIER — his 1837 Zaventem death act, which would give his age, his occupation and\nvery probably his parents, and his marriage to Joanna Theresia Ver Elst, which the\nZaventem registers should hold for roughly 1815–1824. His daughter told the 1846 act that\nshe had never known her grandparents nor where they died, so his parents are not reachable\nthrough her."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"elisabeth_deyn","name":"Elisabeth Deyn","dates":"","confidence":"doc","surname":"Deyn","family":"deyn","died":"","branch":"Thumas","line":"thumas","spouses":[{"id":"arnoldus_bossin","name":"Arnoldus Bossin"}],"source":"Zaventem marriage act nr. 1, 24 February 1846 — Bossin × Peremans; Marriage act nr. 9 — Françiscus Pardon × Anna Maria Bossin, Sint-Stevens-Woluwe, 1 December 1853","note":"Mother of the groom in her son [[guilielmus_bossin]]'s marriage act — Zaventem, act nr.\n1, 24 February 1846 (S19), read as an image. She was present and consenting, and like her\nhusband [[arnoldus_bossin]] she signed with a mark, the act stating she could not write.\n\nThe act gives her no age and no occupation, so this record has neither. The death entry\nrecords only that she was alive on the day.\n\nFRONTIER — her marriage to Arnoldus Bossin, and her surname's spelling. *Deyn* as written\nhere is also found as De(y)n, Deijn and Deyns in Brabant registers, and only her own acts\nwill settle which this family used.\n\nACT READ (2026-07-26): named as mother of the bride in her daughter\n[[anna_maria_bossin]]'s marriage act — Sint-Stevens-Woluwe nr. 9, 1 December 1853 (S22).\n\n**And named as nothing more than a name.** Where the 1846 act had her present, consenting\nand signing with a mark, the 1853 analysis gives her no residence, no trade and no remark,\nwhile giving her husband all three. That is a difference worth recording and not worth\ninterpreting: an absent field in a transcription may mean she was absent, or dead, or\nsimply that the field was not filled. Her death bound therefore still rests on 1846, and\nnothing is inferred from the silence. The register image would settle it."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"elisabeth_langes","name":"Elisabeth Agatha Langes","dates":"","confidence":"sup","surname":"Langes","family":"langes","died":"","occupation":"landbouwster (farmer)","branch":"Thumas","line":"thumas","spouses":[{"id":"jb_vandenbemden","name":"Jan Baptist Van den Bemden"}],"source":"Sint-Stevens-Woluwe marriage act nr. 6, 24 April 1873 — Van den Bemden × Coekelberghs","note":"Mother of the groom in her son [[hendrik_vdb]]'s marriage act — Sint-Stevens-Woluwe nr. 6,\n24 April 1873. A *landbouwster* of Everberg, present at the wedding and consenting to it,\nand recorded as unable to write.\n\nEverberg is where her son was born, in August 1849, so the family was still there\ntwenty-four years later.\n\n**She is a correction, not only an addition.** [[hendrik_vdb]]'s record carried, as an\nunverified Geneanet claim, a mother named *Agatha Langa*. The act names her **Elisabeth\nAgatha** and spells the surname **Langes**. The forename was incomplete and the surname\nwas wrong; both are now as the act has them, and the Geneanet reading is retired.\n\nThe act gives her no age, so this record carries no birth date. The death entry records\nonly that she was alive on the day.\n\n`sup`: an AGATHA act analysis, not the register image.\n\nFRONTIER — the spelling is worth pinning before it is searched on. *Langes* as written here\nalso occurs as Lange, Langhe and Langens in Brabant registers, and the Geneanet reading\n*Langa* is a fourth. Her own marriage or her death act at Everberg would settle it, and a\nsurname that rare is one of the few that can be searched on its own."});
FamilyTree.person({"id":"elisabeth_nn","name":"Elisabeth NN","dates":"","confidence":"sup","sex":"f","branch":"Bundervoet","line":"bundervoet","spouses":[{"id":"livinus_bundervoet","name":"Livinus Bundervoet"}],"source":"paulderidder","note":"First wife of Livinus Bundervoet and mother of Segerius Bundervoet (~1615–1669), so a\ndirect ancestor. Her given name is all that survives: the paulderidder tree records her\nas \"Elisabeth NN\", with no surname, no dates and no parents.\n\nSURNAME NOT KNOWN — and specifically not \"Hovelynck\". Livinus's record here used to\ncall her Elisabeth Hovelynck; the paulderidder tree, which that claim was attributed\nto, gives no surname at all. The surname has been removed rather than carried forward,\nbecause a name that no reachable source states is exactly the kind of detail that\nhardens into fact by being repeated. If another tree does give a surname, it can be\nrestored with that tree cited.\n\nFRONTIER: an Evergem marriage of Livinus Bundervoet in the 1610s would name her in\nfull, and would also settle which of his two marriages came first."});
